Suppose I have a FileWriter class.

Initially, it only writes data to a file.

Later, new requirements come:
- Compress the data
- Encrypt the data
- Log every write

If I use inheritance, I'll end up creating classes like
CompressedFileWriter,
EncryptedFileWriter,
LoggedFileWriter,
CompressedEncryptedFileWriter,
LoggedCompressedEncryptedFileWriter, and so on.

As more optional features are added, the number of subclasses grows rapidly.

Decorator solves this by creating one decorator per feature.
At runtime, I simply wrap the object:

FileWriter
   ↓
Compression
   ↓
Encryption
   ↓
Logging

This avoids class explosion, allows features to be enabled or disabled dynamically, and follows the Open/Closed Principle.

Decorator is needed when behavior is optional, combinable, 
and should be added dynamically at runtime without creating subclasses for every feature combination.


VVVVVIMP
In the Decorator Pattern, every decorator wraps the
previous object, forming a chain of responsibility and
ownership. This ensures each object has exactly one owner,
preventing double deletion and enabling dynamic behavior
addition at runtime.


=========================================================
DECORATOR DESIGN PATTERN
=========================================================

1. WHAT IS IT?
---------------------------------------------------------
Decorator Pattern is a Structural Design Pattern used to
add new functionality (behavior) to an object dynamically
without modifying its existing class.

Instead of changing the original object, it wraps the
object inside another object (Decorator) that adds
additional behavior.

One-Line Definition:
Decorator Pattern dynamically adds behavior to an object
by wrapping it inside another object of the same interface.

=========================================================

2. WHY WAS IT NEEDED?
---------------------------------------------------------

Suppose we have a Pizza.

Possible features:
✔ Cheese
✔ Olives
✔ Mushroom

Using inheritance:

Pizza
├── PizzaWithCheese
├── PizzaWithOlives
├── PizzaWithMushroom
├── PizzaWithCheeseOlives
├── PizzaWithCheeseMushroom
├── PizzaWithOlivesMushroom
└── PizzaWithCheeseOlivesMushroom

Problems:

❌ Too many subclasses
❌ Hard to maintain
❌ Not flexible
❌ Every new feature combination requires another class

This problem is called:

CLASS EXPLOSION

=========================================================

3. HOW DECORATOR SOLVES IT
---------------------------------------------------------

Instead of creating many subclasses, we wrap the object
with decorators.

PlainPizza
      │
      ▼
Cheese Decorator
      │
      ▼
Olives Decorator
      │
      ▼
Mushroom Decorator

Features are added one by one at runtime.

Each decorator adds only its own responsibility.

=========================================================

4. INTENT
---------------------------------------------------------

Attach additional responsibilities to an object
dynamically.

Add features at runtime without modifying the
existing class.

=========================================================

5. STRUCTURE
---------------------------------------------------------

Participants:

1. Component
   - Common interface
   Example:
   Pizza

2. Concrete Component
   - Original object
   Example:
   PlainPizza

3. Decorator
   - Implements same interface
   - Holds a Component object
   Example:
   PizzaDecorator

4. Concrete Decorators
   - Add new functionality
   Example:
   Cheese
   Olives
   Mushroom

=========================================================

6. RELATIONSHIP (IMPORTANT)
---------------------------------------------------------

Decorator uses BOTH Inheritance and Composition.

IS-A Relationship

Cheese IS-A Pizza
Olives IS-A Pizza

(Because decorators inherit from Pizza.)

HAS-A Relationship

Cheese HAS-A Pizza
Olives HAS-A Pizza

(Because decorators hold a Pizza object internally.)

This allows recursive wrapping.

=========================================================

7. KEY IDEA
---------------------------------------------------------

Don't extend classes.

Extend objects.

Objects are wrapped repeatedly.

PlainPizza
      │
      ▼
Cheese
      │
      ▼
Olives
      │
      ▼
Extra Cheese

This creates a chain of decorators.

=========================================================

8. BENEFITS
---------------------------------------------------------

✔ Add behavior dynamically
✔ No modification of base class
✔ Avoid class explosion
✔ Runtime flexibility
✔ Better code reuse
✔ Supports recursive wrapping
✔ Follows Open/Closed Principle

=========================================================

9. DRAWBACKS
---------------------------------------------------------

❌ Many wrapper objects
❌ Deep decorator chains are harder to debug
❌ More object creation
❌ Raw pointers require careful memory management
   (Prefer smart pointers in C++)

=========================================================

10. COMMON REAL-WORLD EXAMPLES
---------------------------------------------------------

✔ Pizza toppings
✔ Coffee + Milk + Sugar
✔ Logging wrappers
✔ Compression wrappers
✔ Encryption wrappers
✔ Authentication middleware
✔ Network protocol layers
✔ GUI widgets
✔ C++ IO Streams (istream, ostream, fstream)

=========================================================

11. INTERVIEW KEY POINTS
---------------------------------------------------------

✔ Structural Design Pattern
✔ Uses Composition over Inheritance
✔ Runtime behavior modification
✔ Decorator implements the same interface
✔ Recursive object wrapping
✔ Multiple decorators can be stacked
✔ Follows Open/Closed Principle

=========================================================

