******VVVVVVVIMP****
=========================================================
IMPORTANT NOTE: HOW DECORATOR OBJECTS SHOULD BE CREATED
=========================================================

A Decorator should NEVER wrap the same base object
multiple times independently.

---------------------------------------------------------
❌ WRONG APPROACH
---------------------------------------------------------

Pizza* p = new PlainPizza();

Cheese* pc = new Cheese(p);
Olives* po = new Olives(p);

Object Structure:

          PlainPizza
          /        \
         /          \
    Cheese        Olives

Problem:

Both Cheese and Olives think they OWN PlainPizza.

Both destructors execute:

delete p;

Scenario:

delete pc;
    ↓
Deletes PlainPizza

delete po;
    ↓
Attempts to delete PlainPizza again

❌ Double Deletion
❌ Undefined Behavior
❌ Possible Crash

---------------------------------------------------------
✅ CORRECT APPROACH
---------------------------------------------------------

Pizza* p = new PlainPizza();

p = new Cheese(p);

p = new Olives(p);

Object Structure:

        Olives
           │
           ▼
        Cheese
           │
           ▼
       PlainPizza

Here,

Olives owns Cheese

Cheese owns PlainPizza

Only ONE outermost object exists.

When we execute:

delete p;

Deletion happens recursively.

delete Olives
      │
      ▼
delete Cheese
      │
      ▼
delete PlainPizza

Every object is deleted exactly once.

---------------------------------------------------------
WHY DOES THIS WORK?
---------------------------------------------------------

Each decorator wraps the PREVIOUS object.

Cheese wraps PlainPizza.

Olives wraps Cheese.

Every decorator owns only ONE object.

This forms a CHAIN of ownership.

---------------------------------------------------------
RULE TO REMEMBER
---------------------------------------------------------

❌ Never decorate the same object separately.

Wrong:

Cheese(p);
Olives(p);

✔ Always decorate the previous decorator.

Correct:

p = new Cheese(p);

p = new Olives(p);

---------------------------------------------------------
INTERVIEW ONE-LINER
---------------------------------------------------------

In the Decorator Pattern, every decorator wraps the
previous object, forming a chain of responsibility and
ownership. This ensures each object has exactly one owner,
preventing double deletion and enabling dynamic behavior
addition at runtime.

